# config/Kconfig.network — Ethernet, TCP/IP stack parameters, ARP, brain link
#
# RFC-0026 Phase C1.  All network constants with defaults matching the
# current codebase values from crates/net/net/src/*.

menu "Network"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ethernet / IP
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config TCP_BUF_SIZE
    int "TCP per-connection receive and send ring (bytes)"
    range 8192 1048576
    default 16384  if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 16384  if DOMAIN_GATEWAY && PROFILE_EDGE
    default 131072
    help
      Size of each of the two static rings of one TCP connection, the
      receive ring and the send ring (crates/net/net/src/tcp.rs rx_buf and
      tx_buf).  Must be a power of two, and at least 8192 so that the
      send ring holds a few full-size segments of headroom for loss
      recovery; crates/core/limits/build.rs rejects anything else.

      Read from .config: tcp.rs TCP_BUF_SIZE = azos_limits::TCP_BUF_SIZE.
      Derived from it in tcp.rs: the send ring (TCP_SND_BUF_SIZE), the
      window of a SYN and of an unscaled connection
      (min(TCP_BUF_SIZE - 1, 65535)), the window-scale shift a SYN offers
      (the smallest that covers TCP_BUF_SIZE - 1: 1 at 131072, 0 at 65536
      or less), the initial ssthresh and the cwnd cap.

      Per profile: 131072 (128 KiB) for edge and embedded, 16384 (16 KiB)
      for fleet.  One connection is size_of::<TcpConn>() =
      2 × TCP_BUF_SIZE + 6,088 B, and the table of TCP_MAX_CONNS of them
      is static (.bss): 8 × 268,232 B for edge, 4 × 268,232 B for
      embedded, 1024 × 38,856 B = 37.9 MiB for fleet (262 MiB at 131072).
      Larger rings improve throughput
      on high-latency links (BDP = RTT × bandwidth).

      Embedded's memory is not budgeted: there is no embedded linker
      script and no gate build row, and the measured image is 5.6 MiB
      against the < 1 MiB SRAM PROFILE_EMBEDDED states.  131072 stays on
      embedded until that budget (target board and RAM, linker script,
      gate build row), which is queued, exists.

      At fleet's 16384 the shift this end offers is 0, so a two-node run
      of a fleet build prints wscale=<peer>/0.

      Cost: 2 x TCP_MAX_CONNS B of RAM per byte of ring (16 B per byte at
      edge's 8 connections: 2 MiB of the 2.05 MiB TCP table is rings), the
      same on riscv64 and aarch64 (measured: +2,097,152 B for 131072 -> 262144
      at 8 connections).  See TCP_MAX_CONNS for the per-connection and total
      figures.  Ceiling: a power of two in 8192..1,048,576 (build.rs refuses
      anything else).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ARP, DNS and UDP tables (wave 11: were literals in crates/net/net)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config ARP_CACHE_SIZE
    int "ARP cache entries"
    range 4 256
    default 64 if DOMAIN_GATEWAY && PROFILE_EDGE
    default 16
    help
      crates/net/net/src/arp.rs, ARP_CACHE_SIZE. DOMAIN_GATEWAY on the
      Edge profile starts it at 64.

      Cost: 24 B of RAM per entry (ARP_TABLE, .bss), the same on riscv64 and
      aarch64: 400 B at the default 16.

config ARP_PENDING_SIZE
    int "Outstanding ARP requests remembered"
    range 2 64
    default 16 if DOMAIN_GATEWAY && PROFILE_EDGE
    default 8
    help
      crates/net/net/src/arp.rs, ARP_PENDING_SIZE: requests whose reply
      is still eligible to be accepted. DOMAIN_GATEWAY on the Edge
      profile starts it at 16.

      Cost: 16 B of RAM per entry (ARP_PENDING, .bss), the same on riscv64 and
      aarch64: 136 B at the default 8.

config DNS_CACHE_SIZE
    int "DNS cache entries"
    range 1 64
    default 16 if DOMAIN_GATEWAY && PROFILE_EDGE
    default 4
    help
      crates/net/net/src/dns.rs, DNS_CACHE_SIZE. DOMAIN_GATEWAY on the
      Edge profile starts it at 16.

      Cost: 80 B of RAM per entry (DNS_CACHE, .bss), the same on riscv64 and
      aarch64: 328 B at the default 4.

config UDP_RX_SLOTS
    int "Datagrams queued per UDP socket"
    range 2 64
    default 16 if DOMAIN_GATEWAY && PROFILE_EDGE
    default 4
    help
      crates/net/net/src/udp.rs, UDP_RX_SLOTS: the receive ring of each
      UDP socket. DOMAIN_GATEWAY on the Edge profile starts it at 16.
      Must be a power of two (the ring index is a mask);
      crates/core/limits/build.rs refuses any other value.

      Cost: 11,904 B of RAM per slot (the UDP table, .bss), the same on riscv64
      and aarch64: 8 UDP sockets (UDP_MAX_SOCKETS, a literal) x one 1,488-byte
      datagram slot each.  47,912 B for the whole UDP table at the default 4
      slots; 64 slots (the range limit) would be 761,856 B of slots.  It does not
      depend on MAX_SOCKETS.

endmenu # Network
